# (quote, source). Public domain only.
QUOTES: list[tuple[str, str]] = [
    ("And so, as Tiny Tim observed, God bless Us, Every One!", "Charles Dickens, A Christmas Carol (1843)"),
    ("I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year.", "Charles Dickens, A Christmas Carol (1843)"),
    ("I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me.", "Charles Dickens, A Christmas Carol (1843)"),
    ("I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a school-boy.", "Charles Dickens, A Christmas Carol (1843)"),
    ("Marley was dead: to begin with. There is no doubt whatever about that.", "Charles Dickens, A Christmas Carol (1843)"),
    ("There is nothing in the world so irresistibly contagious as laughter and good-humour.", "Charles Dickens, A Christmas Carol (1843)"),
    ("It was always said of him, that he knew how to keep Christmas well, if any man alive possessed the knowledge.", "Charles Dickens, A Christmas Carol (1843)"),
    ("Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were, all, my business.", "Charles Dickens, A Christmas Carol (1843)"),
    ("A good time: a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time.", "Charles Dickens, A Christmas Carol (1843)"),
    ("No space of regret can make amends for one life's opportunity misused.", "Charles Dickens, A Christmas Carol (1843)"),
    ("Happy, happy Christmas, that can win us back to the delusions of our childish days.", "Charles Dickens, The Pickwick Papers (1836)"),
    ("Fear not: for, behold, I bring you good tidings of great joy, which shall be to all people.", "Luke 2:10 (KJV)"),
    ("For unto you is born this day in the city of David a Saviour, which is Christ the Lord.", "Luke 2:11 (KJV)"),
    ("Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good will toward men.", "Luke 2:14 (KJV)"),
    ("For unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given: and the government shall be upon his shoulder.", "Isaiah 9:6 (KJV)"),
    ("And his name shall be called Wonderful, Counsellor, The mighty God, The everlasting Father, The Prince of Peace.", "Isaiah 9:6 (KJV)"),
    ("When they saw the star, they rejoiced with exceeding great joy.", "Matthew 2:10 (KJV)"),
    ("They shall call his name Emmanuel, which being interpreted is, God with us.", "Matthew 1:23 (KJV)"),
    ("And laid him in a manger; because there was no room for them in the inn.", "Luke 2:7 (KJV)"),
    ("And the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us, full of grace and truth.", "John 1:14 (KJV)"),
    ("But Mary kept all these things, and pondered them in her heart.", "Luke 2:19 (KJV)"),
    ("They presented unto him gifts; gold, and frankincense, and myrrh.", "Matthew 2:11 (KJV)"),
    ("And there were in the same country shepherds abiding in the field, keeping watch over their flock by night.", "Luke 2:8 (KJV)"),
    ("Arise, shine; for thy light is come, and the glory of the Lord is risen upon thee.", "Isaiah 60:1 (KJV)"),
    ("Twas the night before Christmas, when all through the house not a creature was stirring, not even a mouse.", "Clement C. Moore, A Visit from St. Nicholas (1823)"),
    ("The stockings were hung by the chimney with care, in hopes that St. Nicholas soon would be there.", "Clement C. Moore, A Visit from St. Nicholas (1823)"),
    ("The children were nestled all snug in their beds, while visions of sugar-plums danced in their heads.", "Clement C. Moore, A Visit from St. Nicholas (1823)"),
    ("Happy Christmas to all, and to all a good-night!", "Clement C. Moore, A Visit from St. Nicholas (1823)"),
    ("He had a broad face and a little round belly, that shook when he laughed, like a bowlful of jelly.", "Clement C. Moore, A Visit from St. Nicholas (1823)"),
    ("I heard the bells on Christmas Day their old, familiar carols play, and wild and sweet the words repeat of peace on earth, good-will to men!", "Henry Wadsworth Longfellow (1863)"),
    ("God is not dead, nor doth He sleep! The Wrong shall fail, the Right prevail, with peace on earth, good-will to men!", "Henry Wadsworth Longfellow (1863)"),
    ("Yes, Virginia, there is a Santa Claus. He exists as certainly as love and generosity and devotion exist.", "The New York Sun (1897)"),
    ("Christmas won't be Christmas without any presents, grumbled Jo, lying on the rug.", "Louisa May Alcott, Little Women (1868)"),
    ("At Christmas play and make good cheer, for Christmas comes but once a year.", "Thomas Tusser (1557)"),
    ("Silent night, holy night, all is calm, all is bright.", "Silent Night (carol, 1818)"),
    ("Joy to the world, the Lord is come! Let earth receive her King.", "Isaac Watts, Joy to the World (1719)"),
    ("Hark! the herald angels sing, Glory to the newborn King; peace on earth, and mercy mild, God and sinners reconciled.", "Charles Wesley (1739)"),
    ("O holy night, the stars are brightly shining; it is the night of the dear Saviour's birth.", "O Holy Night (carol, 1847)"),
    ("O little town of Bethlehem, how still we see thee lie! Above thy deep and dreamless sleep the silent stars go by.", "Phillips Brooks (1868)"),
    ("Deck the halls with boughs of holly, fa la la la la, la la la la. Tis the season to be jolly.", "Deck the Halls (traditional carol)"),
    ("We wish you a merry Christmas and a happy New Year. Good tidings we bring to you and your kin.", "We Wish You a Merry Christmas (traditional carol)"),
    ("Dashing through the snow in a one-horse open sleigh, o'er the fields we go, laughing all the way.", "James Lord Pierpont, Jingle Bells (1857)"),
    ("God rest ye merry, gentlemen, let nothing you dismay, remember Christ our Saviour was born on Christmas Day.", "God Rest Ye Merry, Gentlemen (traditional carol)"),
    ("Away in a manger, no crib for a bed, the little Lord Jesus laid down his sweet head.", "Away in a Manger (carol, 1885)"),
    ("O come, all ye faithful, joyful and triumphant, O come ye, O come ye to Bethlehem.", "O Come, All Ye Faithful (carol, 18th century)"),
    ("It came upon the midnight clear, that glorious song of old, from angels bending near the earth, to touch their harps of gold.", "Edmund Sears (1849)"),
    ("The first Noel the angel did say was to certain poor shepherds in fields as they lay.", "The First Noel (traditional carol)"),
    ("Good King Wenceslas looked out on the feast of Stephen, when the snow lay round about, deep and crisp and even.", "John Mason Neale (1853)"),
    ("We three kings of Orient are; bearing gifts we traverse afar.", "John Henry Hopkins Jr. (1857)"),
    ("O Christmas tree, O Christmas tree, how lovely are thy branches!", "O Christmas Tree (traditional carol)"),
    ("On the first day of Christmas my true love sent to me a partridge in a pear tree.", "The Twelve Days of Christmas (traditional)"),
    ("Angels we have heard on high, sweetly singing o'er the plains.", "Angels We Have Heard on High (traditional carol)"),
    ("Go, tell it on the mountain, over the hills and everywhere; go, tell it on the mountain that Jesus Christ is born.", "Go, Tell It on the Mountain (spiritual)"),
    ("Up on the housetop reindeer pause, out jumps good old Santa Claus.", "Benjamin Hanby (1864)"),
    ("Over the river and through the wood, to grandfather's house we go.", "Lydia Maria Child (1844)"),
    ("The holly and the ivy, when they are both full grown, of all the trees that are in the wood, the holly bears the crown.", "The Holly and the Ivy (traditional carol)"),
    ("In the bleak midwinter, frosty wind made moan, earth stood hard as iron, water like a stone.", "Christina Rossetti (1872)"),
    ("Love came down at Christmas, love all lovely, love divine.", "Christina Rossetti (1885)"),
    ("What Child is this, who, laid to rest, on Mary's lap is sleeping?", "William Chatterton Dix (1865)"),
    ("Angels from the realms of glory, wing your flight o'er all the earth.", "James Montgomery (1816)"),
    ("Christmas is the season for kindling the fire of hospitality in the hall, the genial flame of charity in the heart.", "Washington Irving, The Sketch Book (1820)"),
    ("Here we come a-wassailing among the leaves so green.", "Here We Come A-wassailing (traditional carol)"),
]

SCRIPTURE = [q for q in QUOTES if "(KJV)" in q[1]]
